cific to survival mode  though I would recommend that people  play normal mode first to explain those  to normal mode is described as a chill  exploration experience and it's the mode  that no man's sky started out with you  land on a planet with no recollection of  how you got there even though your ship  seems to recognize you and a toss with  scouring that plant to look for  resources to fix your ship so you can  take off into the stars following a  series of mysterious messages that are  guiding you somewhere the storyline is  interesting enough and worth pursuing  and it's really cool that you can pursue  it in  you play with other people doing it at  the same time but I've largely been  creating my own stories in a way that I  feel is similar to Minecraft Survival  mode follows that same basic concept  just without the chill  so survival mode gives you limited  resources increased hazards and stronger  more aggressive enemies even finding  your ship after you first spawn is hard  you can't just walk there because the  environmental hazards will on most  planets quickly kill you so you have to  immediately figure out how to and what  to mine before you get going it feels  very much like a traditional survival  game and while at least on your first  planet I don't think it innovates hugely  on that genre but I get a feeling from  no man's sky that I don't think I've  ever felt before that is of genuine  space exploration and I really think the  new third-person mode helped me with  that I played almost entirely in  third-person though you can play in  first-person if you want to the first  planet I set foot on was harsh and I  completely believe complaints that if  you spawn on a bad planet there's a  chance that it'll feel impossible for  you to survive and that is a little  unfair but the thing that gets me is  that it feels real yes if I were to  explore outer space I could come across  a planet that is so incompatible with  human life and simply doesn't contain  the resources needed for survival and I  love that about it I want to liken it to  moments in the legend is all the breath  of the wild but on a universal scale  there are some things that you'll come  across that are absolutely amazing so  much so that I don't even want to tell  you what they are on a related note  don't read the patch notes but you might  be flying a ship and notice a planet  comprised of interesting colors you get  closer and you start noticing  interesting patterns on the ground you  get closer to those and realize there's  a giant crashed ship with a bunch of  unopened cago take a look at that cargo  and it needs some mind and door repairs  to be opened you figure out how to do it  you open it and there's something inside  of there that you've never seen before  bits worth thousands of units if you  sell it on a space station but since the  cargo hull was broken it also leaks a  ton of radiation which you want  expecting and you immediately have to  run away maybe as you're running away  you come across an alien artifact that  teaches you a single word of the  language of the gek an alien race you've  met before but never been able to  communicate with now you're learning  about races and their languages on top  of everything else and moments like this  commonly exist in  man sky now on top of hugely improved  terrain generation that makes most  planets really beautiful and  consistently interesting and it just  keeps up that sense of discovery through  every facet of the game and it is  multifaceted now to my second planet was  compared to the first heavenly it was  colorful then we know environmental  hazards except for the occasional toxic  cave and sentinels that would kill me if  they managed to scan me something that I  will admit is mightily annoying because  every large planet seems to have  aggressive sentinels but I digress lack  of environmental hazards meant I didn't  have to worry about hazard support so I  made this my first mining plant because  it was so full of resources I built up  an inventory of oxygen life-support  packs ion batteries sodium all the  things I would need if I encountered  another planet like the first one I also  built a base fit with the teleporter so  I could easily get back to it if I found  another planet that had equals  survivability and benefits and it's like  I became this intergalactic mining  extraordinaire with houses on several  planets a significant amount of money  that I was making by mining and refining  and then selling rare resources I didn't  need and an efficiency for minimizing  time on dangerous planets outside of  landing scanning for resources that  might be valuable for me to sell and new  life-forms or you know as much  exploration as satisfied my curiosity so  you're combining space exploration which  is amazing in itself with much more  ongoing personal decisions and as a  result it feels fulfilling in ways that  it just didn't back at launch speaking  of which I think I just personally enjoy  it so much more on survival mode because  the stakes are higher and I say that as  someone who generally does really enjoy  challenging games there's totally a  chance that you'd prefer normal mode if  you don't want that increased risk of  death and the ability to lose all of  your items but I still find normal mode  a little too simple it's definitely  chill they're right about that but I  think because of the lack of challenge  and the game still is procedurally  generated sometimes things feel very  lifeless sometimes some planets you come  across ah uninteresting even though  that's a lot less frequent that it  wasn't launched and I think that just  because of that without a little more  challenge I don't feel like I'm  accomplishing enough for it to really  grab me even if there is far more to do  and far more direct  then when the game first came out also  there's a permadeath mode too if you're  into that it combines the difficulty of  survival mode with you know permanent  death there's also a creative mode where  you completely can't die and you have  unlimited resources which is great for  building and learning to build which I  will add I did a lot of in survival mode  on the Xbox one and ps4 I'm not sure  about PC building actual structures is a  little awkward in that sometimes it's  hard to direct where exactly you want to  put a certain thing but it's effectively  required if you want to hide yourself  from extreme weather storms and it's  convenient to have a structure that you  know houses a lot of the items you only  to survive you can even build farms  things what we replenish your oxygen or  your sodium or even literally farm them  like a device that generates oxygen as  you get later into the progression  system there's enough variety and things  to build it it also just feels like  you're always learning always  discovering new things always on the  heels of the next cool device you'll be  able to craft and then you can approach  finding the materials at your own pace  crafting adds an extra objective for you  to piss you on top of already trying to  upgrade or get new ships or vehicles or  mining wear items to sell on the  Galactic market or taking jobs to make  money or discover new planets or  whatever else the options are that  finally exists in no man's sky now my  biggest complaint I think is that mining  can feel pretty repetitive there are  only so many times I can know that I  need to mine rocks before I start going  okay let's go find some more rocks I  guess almost all of your equipment is  upgradeable so you can make that system  faster and more efficient but you still  are spending a lot of time mining and  without the luxury of coming across  underground mine shops or what have you  it loses its appeal a much miner get it  minor complaint is that some items  require really specific aiming like  you'll think you're looking right at it  but you won't be prompted to pick it up  or it can't be mined that's an annoying  one because there's no way to really  learn what's correct sometimes it works  sometimes it just doesn't and when  you're mining for a long time that gets  annoying the UI specifically the menu  can also feel a little bit cluttered  when your inventory is full there's a  lot of information on screen and often  the easiest way to find the recipe you  might need to build something is to find  it in your inventory which can feel like  an unnecessary multi-step process I also  need to mention that I encountered  frequent framerate drops on both Xbox  one and ps4 but I did not encounter any  major bugs or crashes or any save file  issues like a lot of other people have  fortunately but apparently all of these  issues are being patched up a cool stuff  that you might care about that I feel  like I'm running out of time to talk  about you can fight pirate so basically  be a pirate and dogfight space battles  and upgrade for combat orientated ships  if that's what you're about I tended to  avoid pretty much all that you can build  up a fleet of ships and send them on  missions for you all the while building  up your own captain ship to  progressively get cooler and cooler  there's a basic character customisation  system if you really want to change your  avatar including it down to species and  the designs are all surprisingly really  different and creative honestly in 2018  there's just so much stuff to do in no  man's sky and I thoroughly enjoy all of  the stuff I'm choosing to do and that's  the beauty of it there's a lot of  variety so finding what works for you  give it a shot  I'm not saying everyone's going to like  the game because it's never going to be  for everyone of course I'm not going to  forgive all of the failures and I don't  think the industry will forget how that  all went down and it was very ugly for  everyone involved but hey I'm glad they  spent so much time walking on it so I  get spend so much time playing it for  what it is today  with that everyone let me know if you  have any more questions let me know if  you've been playing it I would love to  hear what you guys think you know  especially considering most people  including myself were negative on it  when it first came out it's just a  fascinating journey and yeah I'm Alana  and I will see you guys next time

